  "meta": {
    "title": "aⁿbⁿ ∪ aⁿb²ⁿ — context-free, not deterministic",
    "blurb": "Each half is a deterministic language, and their union is not. Reading the a’s, a machine must already know whether to count them once or twice — so it guesses, one branch per answer.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbbb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbb",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbb",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ba",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "nondeterminism",
        "deterministic-cfl",
        "closure"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "The deterministic context-free languages are not closed under union, and this is the standard witness. **aⁿbⁿ** and **aⁿb²ⁿ** each have a DPDA — see **aⁿb²ⁿ** in this library — but a deterministic machine reading the a's has to decide how much to push per a before it has seen a single b, and whichever choice it makes, some word proves it wrong.\n\n(The proof: a DPDA is in one definite configuration after reading aⁿbⁿ, whatever follows. Run it that far, then keep running it on n more b's relabelled as c's, accepting when it would accept — and you have a PDA for aⁿbⁿcⁿ, which does not exist.)\n\nWith a guess at the start, it is two machines glued together, sharing their accepting state."
    }
  }
}
